  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 251 PM MDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nSynoptically, as the previous day's system moves out of the County \nWarning Area (CWA), our upper level flow will largely remain from \nthe west to northwest though Tuesday. During this period, the jet \nstream remains to the north near the US/Canada border throughout \nthe weekend. Over the Four Corner region, a weak upper level \nridge is likely to set up starting Friday morning and lingering \nthrough late next week. There is some uncertainty in the upper \nlevel pattern for Tuesday. Guidance currently indicated either a\nweak shortwave or an upper level trough moving through the \narea. Currently, the Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) is \ncurrently forecast at 5-10%, however the shortwave could \nincrease precipitation chances depending on its strength.\n\nOverall, temperatures for the weekend into early next week look to \nstay under 80 degrees until around Tuesday. Friday looks to be the \ncoolest day, with highs in the 60s. By Monday and Tuesday, highs \ncreep back into the low to mid 80s for the majority of the Tri-State \narea. Precipitation chances for the weekend and early work week \nremain low each day, with PoPs currently around 5-10%. Both the NBM \nand LREF ensemble means show less than 500 J/kg of CAPE daily. \nHowever, as noted previously, the passing shortwave on Tuesday could \nbring a slight bump in PoPs. \n\nFriday through Sunday afternoon, sustained winds and gusts are \nforecast to remain mostly below 25 mph. Starting Sunday \nafternoon/evening winds should begin to transition to come from the \nsouth for the remainder of the long term period. Monday is currently \nforecast to be the windiest day of the period as 850 mb jet \ndevelops. While guidance disagrees on the exact location of area\nof the highest gusts, which could be in the range of 30-45 mph.\nCurrently the NBM favors the Colorado counties as well as areas\nnorth of I-70 and west of HWY 83 for the highest gusts. Whereas\nthe LREF favors the higher gusts to be over the majority of the\nCWA. Currently Fire Weather concerns remain fairly low due to \nthe Relative Humidity values staying above 30% and soil moisture\nlikely remaining fairly saturated from the recent \nprecipitation.",
